# Header Files

This directory contains the project's header files.

Header files (`.h`) declare functions, types, constants, and macros that are shared across multiple source files. To use a header, include it in a source file located in the `src/` directory with the C preprocessor:

```c
// src/main.c

#include "header.h"

int main(void)
{
    // ...
}
```

Using header files avoids duplicating declarations across source files. Instead of copying the same declarations into multiple files, they are maintained in a single location. This makes the codebase easier to maintain, reduces the risk of inconsistencies, and ensures that all source files automatically use the latest declarations after recompilation.

By convention, C header files use the `.h` extension.

For more information about header files and the `#include` directive, see the GCC documentation:

* Include Syntax
* Include Operation
* Once-Only Headers
* Computed Includes

https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/cpp/Header-Files.html
